## Google sign-in

### Status
Not started.

### GitHub Issue
#79

### Summary
Add "Sign in with Google" as an alternative authentication method alongside the existing
ASP.NET Core Identity local username/password flow. Google-authenticated users link to
the same `ApplicationUser` record type used today — this is an additional login method,
not a parallel account system.

### Motivation
Some users would rather authenticate with an existing Google account than create and
remember a separate WhenWorks username/password. ASP.NET Core Identity already supports
external login providers; the scaffolded `Login.cshtml` view already renders a section
for external providers (`Model.ExternalLogins`) and posts to `./ExternalLogin`, but no
Google provider is registered and no `ExternalLogin` page exists yet to handle the
callback.

### Proposed Changes
1. Add the `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Google` NuGet package to
   `WhenWorksWeb.csproj`.
2. Register Google authentication in `Program.cs` via
   `builder.Services.AddAuthentication().AddGoogle(...)`, reading `ClientId`/`ClientSecret`
   from configuration (`dotnet user-secrets` locally, environment variables/App Service
   configuration in production) — never hardcoded or committed to `appsettings.json`.
3. Add `Areas/Identity/Pages/Account/ExternalLogin.cshtml` + `.cshtml.cs`, following the
   standard ASP.NET Core Identity scaffolded pattern:
   - `OnGetAsync`/challenge redirect to Google.
   - `OnGetCallbackAsync` handles the return trip: if `ExternalLoginSignInManager`
     recognizes the Google identity as already linked to an `ApplicationUser`, sign in
     directly.
   - If it's a new Google identity, fall through to a confirmation step that collects the
     app's required custom fields (`DisplayName`, `Color`) before calling
     `_userManager.CreateAsync(user)` + `AddLoginAsync(user, info)` — mirroring the
     `DisplayName`/`Color` collection already in `Register.cshtml.cs`, since those
     properties have no meaningful default a Google profile can supply.
   - Explicitly set `CreatedAt`/`LastActiveAt` when constructing the new `ApplicationUser`
     (`Register.cshtml.cs`'s `Activator.CreateInstance<ApplicationUser>()` currently leaves
     these `required` properties at their CLR default rather than actually populating them —
     don't replicate that gap in the new code path; flag it separately rather than fixing
     it here, since it's pre-existing and out of scope for this feature).
4. This will be the first area code under `Areas/Identity` that isn't purely scaffolded
   boilerplate — per CLAUDE.md, `Areas/Identity` changes need explicit approval, which this
   spec is seeking.
5. Update `Login.cshtml`'s existing external-provider loop only if the scaffolded rendering
   needs visual/styling adjustment to match the current site styling pass; the
   provider-loop markup and `asp-page="./ExternalLogin"` post target already exist and
   should not need structural changes.

### Acceptance Criteria
- A "Sign in with Google" button appears on the login page once a Google provider is
  configured.
- A user authenticating with Google for the first time is prompted for `DisplayName` and
  `Color` (the app's required custom fields) before their `ApplicationUser` is created.
- A returning user who previously linked Google signs in directly without re-entering
  those fields.
- Local username/password login continues to work unchanged.
- Google `ClientId`/`ClientSecret` are never committed to source control.

### Out of Scope
- A "manage external logins" page (`Areas/Identity/Pages/Account/Manage/ExternalLogins.cshtml`)
  for linking/unlinking Google from an existing local-password account after the fact —
  the issue asks only for Google as a sign-in method, not full external-login management.
  Worth a separate feature entry if wanted later.
- Other OAuth providers (Facebook, Microsoft, etc.) — Google only, per the issue.
- Any change to the local username/password flow itself.
- Fixing the pre-existing `CreatedAt`/`LastActiveAt` gap in `Register.cshtml.cs`'s local
  registration path (noted above) — flagged for a separate bug entry, not fixed here.
